at ms keegan always great to see you do  hope you're well just apologies we can't  be in the same studio during this this  festive week um  let's start with uh with a personal  admission from all of us it's not  feeling very very christmasy uh this  week is it  but the prime minister has said that we  will still be allowed to go with our  christmas parties or on what basis has  he come to that decision  well on the on the basis that you know  we're taking sort of balanced and  proportionate um  approach to it and you know people can  still go ahead and have their christmas  i would disagree actually i had a family  meal yesterday um and it did feel very  christmasy particularly my sister made  it over from the states so uh yeah it's  feeling christmassy families are getting  together sometime for the first time in  nearly three years  i don't know maybe i'm just playing into  the stereotype of the scottish  conversion this morning but let's let's  talk about the data  let's talk about the data though do we  and do we know do we have a figure for  how many people are in hospital at the  moment with omicron  yeah there's a 129 people in hospital  with omicron right now  the the reason i ask and i'm sure you  will be aware of this on the daily brief  the daily update the omicron update it  did suggest that there were some issues  around i.t reporting those those issues  have now been dealt with  well look we're getting data from all  across the country from every hospital  which is being fed up on a daily and  sometimes hourly basis so you know  there's obviously some lags in certain  certain bits of the data but that's the  data that we're collecting 129 people as  of yesterday and 14 deaths with omicron  just in terms of of what is happening do  you feel like we are approaching the  peak of omicron or perhaps have even  peaked but paul hunter from the  university of east anglia suggesting  that numbers may have plateaued we would  have been expecting perhaps if we go on  the growth rate from two weeks ago  perhaps 200 000 cases a day  instead we're reporting around about 90  000.  um no we haven't been given that  information we're watching the data very  very carefully i mean you had three  meetings yesterday where we're all  discussing the data and taking in those  inputs so we don't know that yet um but  we are looking at the wave and we're  looking at all the data and uh we'll  we'll tell you when we think we've  passed peak  so we haven't yet seen a major rise in  hospitalizations if we generalise across  the country but there is there is an  expectation that one is coming though  isn't it  well you know the things that are  unknown which i think we've said a  number of times that they're still  working on is the severity  of the disease and also the impact of  the vaccination and now more importantly  the vaccination plus the booster we know  the booster gives  a huge increase in defense which is why  we're urging everybody and thank you so  much to the nearly 30 million people  who've come forward but for everybody  else if you haven't had your first dose  it really is never too late come forward  start to get yourself and others around  you protected  just in terms of the of the negotiations  well and it feels like there are  negotiations going on around the cabinet  table your boss sanji javid none other  than the health secretary understood to  have been in favor of bringing in  restrictions earlier he told his  colleagues that doing nothing is an is  an active decision but he was he was out  voted do you think he was correct to be  out voted around that table  look you know in the around the cabinet  table obviously you've got to get  everybody with the different views  looking from a different perspective one  will look from health one will look from  the economy one will look from transport  etc and so that's why you have a cabinet  government so you can get all of that  diversity of views and then take a  balanced approach to risk and it is a  difficult decision we do not have all  the information that you would like to  have at your fingertips in particular  as you were talking about earlier the  severity of the disease so it is a  difficult balance but we think we've got  the balance right you know saying to the  country we wanted to lock down et cetera  when you've got those kind of figures  would wouldn't look proportionate so  we're trying to be balanced we're trying  to be proportionate but most importantly  we are monitoring the data um on a on a  daily basis a couple of times a day  actually we get new inputs of data and  that's what we'll continue to do we'll  be working all over christmas and we'll  continue to do that so was the first  minister of scotland wrong to go to go  further than the prime minister then  does she know something that he doesn't  i thought they were working from the  same data set  they are and they have been all the way  through the pandemic but obviously the  devolved administrations have taken  different approaches uh at times and you  know that's their that's their um you  know their authority to be able to do  that um you know people look at the data  the data is no different but i guess uh  the way they analyze the risk could be  different or they look at their  population you know we we've taken a  look at it and we've basically said you  know the hospitalizations the deaths  where we are right now as you said uh  the rising cases whilst the case numbers  are very high um you know they haven't  yet uh reached what was estimated only a  week ago  sure but but just in terms of further  restrictions  it is fair isn't it to say that it's a  question of when and not it  well obviously we've said we have to  keep that option open because you know  we are looking at cases that are going  up at the moment and we are looking at  uh hospitalization which is going up i  was on the program last week and it was  10 then now it's 129. so obviously we do  have to  reserve that option and we won't  hesitate we've said that we will not  hesitate if it's required to keep people  safe but right now the balanced and  proportionate response is we believe the  one that we've put in place  a big announcement in terms of of  isolation for those that are fully  vaccinated at the very least their  self-isolation periods be cut from ten  days to seven  what is the science behind that why are  we now confident that people can be out  you know mixing in the community again  at day seven rather than day ten  well what we've done is looked at  whether serial testing on day six and  day seven uh how that uh the risk of  that uh versus weight until day ten and  effectively they've said it's equivalent  risk if you have day six and day seven  negative tests is equivalent risk to  having another three days in your  bedroom or whatever  but you are asking people who do leave  their homes on  on day seven having had those two  lateral flow tests you are asking them  to minimize social contact you're asking  them to to avoid mixing if at all  possible why is that necessary if it's  safe for them to leave the house on day  seven  we're saying that for everybody we're  saying to everybody look be cautious if  you're going somewhere i mean we we had  a family meal yesterday everyone took a  lateral flow test before make sure you  go to somewhere that's well ventilated  be cautious we're saying that to  everybody you know there's a lot of  unknowns here we still you know don't  have uh all the data that we want um so  be cautious be cautious to enjoy your  christmas  as much as anything else is this perhaps  a means by which we can help out the the  really significant staffing pressures  that there are in the health service at  the moment i mean the bma estimating  between 32 and 130 000 it could be off  work by christmas  yeah i mean that's part of it keeping  the economy going keeping the supply  chains going keeping the nhs going and  keeping yourself going you know  everybody who's uh involved in in  in jobs uh right now  for sure that is part of of looking at  whether uh people can uh with the with  the equivalent risk can uh get back to  work earlier so yes that is part of the  equation  a big package you know inspired the  chance there yesterday a billion pounds  to go to the hospitality sector six  thousand pounds for every individual  business it isn't that a little bit like  trying to fight third world famine with  a packet of frozen peas i mean i spoke  to one venue owner yesterday who said  their daily takings at this time of year  are close to six thousand pounds  yeah sure i mean look what we're not  doing is looking to try and you know  you know  compensate all of that what we're  looking to do is to provide a a security  blanket for businesses like we did all  the way through the pandemic i mean  we've spent 407 billion pounds on on on  businesses and business support so you  know we're just trying to do that for  this period of time we know it's really  unfortunate obviously hitting just  before christmas it is really  unfortunate so to provide that lifeline  and hopefully once we can all get back  out the great british public will do  what they do and they'll support uh all  of these businesses and get back to the  theaters and get back to restaurants  sure but  you described it as a security blanket  if it is such it is pretty threadbare i  mean tim romney chief executive of best  western he says it's just so  disappointing and underwhelming in every  sense it doesn't go far enough opinion  it doesn't go deep enough and it's too  little too late i mean why have we not  reintroduced furlough  well because people are still working i  mean i went out for a family meal  yesterday you know there was the pub was  not every table was full but most of it  was you know people are still going out  and people are still enjoying themselves  so you know we're trying to get that  balance again but actually most  businesses i've spoken to are actually  very appreciative of this support so  obviously you found one there doesn't  think it's enough you'll always find  that but you know this is this is a  billion pounds that we have just  announced and it is there to support  businesses across our country hopefully  for a short period of time until we can  um you know get over this latest uh  variant and the wave of uh disruption  that it's caused  but just in terms of wages because that  clearly has been something that many in  the in the industry have been calling  out for assistance the movement  statutory sick pay helps the funds but  it doesn't help the one in six  hospitality workers perhaps as many as  240 000 people who don't qualify for it  well i mean it's there again for those  smaller firms um to make sure that  people can self-isolate for up to two  weeks the government will pay um and you  know it's it's it's been very welcomed i  think from from most of the businesses  that uh i've spoken to him from what  i've heard as well you know this is  something again we're dealing with  uncertainty we've responded really  quickly to this latest uncertainty we  hope it doesn't last too long but that  is uh as you know a billion pounds  that's been announced and it is not  something that should be dismissed it's  something that i think largely is  welcomed  minister let's let's return to the top  of our interview  where i you know self-describe myself as  a bit of a grumpy scot perhaps you can  lift my mood i mean in your mind  on the basis of the the limits that  people are already putting on their own  activity as you mentioned you know  you're in a restaurant yesterday and not  all the tables were used in your mind if  we carry on on this path and people keep  doing what they are doing is there any  chance  any chance whatsoever that we might not  see that circuit breaker lockdown that  frankly everyone is predicting  again you know we as i've said we're  waiting for data on the severity we'll  still have to wait to see  um you know where we land on that but um  we can't really say you know we've said  is up to christmas we're fine looking at  the data looking at the the numbers we  have at the moment but of course we have  to look at where this virus goes where  this variant goes so we have to look at  that data i can't tell you in advance of  getting that data but you should be  cheerful because we're doing a lot more  than we could last year we're able to  see our family as i said i have my  sister and her family over from the  states we haven't seen them for nearly  three years so lots of people are  getting to see family members that they  haven't seen for a long time and lots of  us will get together with our loved ones  at christmas  julian keegan lovely to have you on the  program and you make sure you have a  lovely christmas when it comes  you too happy christmas and cheer up

